/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1007 "Mathematical Foundations II" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1008 "Mathematical Foundations II is the second course in a sequence specially crafted for adults seeking the most direct path to prepare for university math courses. Building on the basic algebra covered in Mathematical Foundations I , students in Mathematical Foundations II generalize their understanding of algebra to more advanced functions (including trigonometry) and become acquainted with limits and derivatives in calculus. Upon completing the course, students will be prepared for Mathematical Foundations III , the final stepping stone in preparing for university-level math." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1009 "Master the algebra of advanced functions including quadratics, logarithms, and trigonometry. Dive deep into the theory of polynomials, learn the basics of limits, derivatives, and integrals from calculus, and explore a variety of concepts from higher math including complex numbers, vectors, probability, and statistics." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1012 111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1013 "MF2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046647 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "b5d8b420-892a-5b6a-8150-ba3c70c4d779" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049108 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049108 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049108 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049108 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049108 #edb/ref 1031 "Finding Areas Using the Cross Product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049108 #edb/ref 1033 1299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049108 #edb/ref 1034 0.9648025277048662 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049108 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "db18b7ba-2bde-56e6-a3f9-5471d37eae2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049449 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049449 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049449 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049449 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049449 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049449 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049449 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049449 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428287 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049449 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428288 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049449 #edb/ref 1031 "Calculating Areas of Right Triangles Using Trigonometry" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049449 #edb/ref 1033 1608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049449 #edb/ref 1034 1.08349643467094 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049449 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "f65efa00-a9e9-5306-b53a-84a278412a36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050149 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050149 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050149 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050149 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050149 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050149 #edb/ref 1031 "The Area of a General Triangle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050149 #edb/ref 1033 1275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050149 #edb/ref 1034 0.8919880061792005 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050149 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "30edc058-70f2-5a72-9472-b7c90e677423" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050847 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050847 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050847 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050847 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050847 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050847 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050847 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050847 #edb/ref 1031 "Special Trigonometric Ratios" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050847 #edb/ref 1033 765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050847 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6b5cc7c5-f3ba-5a78-aceb-e5c263dbe224" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105560 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105560 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ below. Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n![](images/dc/dc625408598aa3cbfda44ec877c9a26ab563dee9fcf757192fcf2cf8411f606a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105560 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "62405619-31ca-5403-aa45-360e6b3fbd59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105560 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\dfrac{1}{2}ab\\sin{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\angle{C}$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $b.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =CB=58 \\\\ b & =CA=40 \\\\ m∠C & ={31}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\sin{31^{\\circ}} = 0.515\\,038\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 58\\cdot 40\\operatorname{sin}⁡{31}^{∘} \\\\  & =1160\\cdot (0.515\\,038) \\\\  & ≈597.44,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105561 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3294" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105561 #edb/ref 1071 "Rounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$\n\n![](images/bc/bc9f32a9c0e8b042498a72e808a29274bd505e786d8ec43d0e0bc9888fdf6e44.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105561 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b41b2354-c339-5398-ab43-5886b8447c1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105561 #edb/ref 1074 "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle ADC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/ac/acd2e923a65d0821cd59ed8b8274ceb5fc38def896467e37c8b1d454f958699e.png)\n\n First, we find the area for $\\triangle ABC,$ and get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABC} & =\\frac{1}{2}(42)(35)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{36}^{∘} \\\\  & =735\\operatorname{sin}⁡{36}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈432.022\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $\\triangle ADC,$ and get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ADC} & =\\frac{1}{2}(20)(18)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{80}^{∘} \\\\  & =180\\operatorname{sin}⁡{80}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈177.265\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD.$ We get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABC}+{A}_{ADC} \\\\  & =432.022+177.265 \\\\  & ≈609\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $609.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105562 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105562 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of $ABCD$ is $300\\, \\text{cm}^2.$  What is the area of $\\triangle ABC$ rounded to the nearest integer?\n\n![](images/c0/c06ffb123bdd24c3f83419767b6d1578223b8093089b79f91aae8f1fc6121305.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "472216c8-da7d-58fb-9056-e71d5e33460d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105562 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by finding the area of $\\triangle ACD.$ We get \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot DA\\cdot DC\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡D \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(25)(32)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =400\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =375.877... \\\\  & ≈376\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ and $\\triangle ACD$ gives the area of $\\triangle ABC.$ Therefore, \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABC} & ={A}_{ACD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =376-300 \\\\  & =76\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105579 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4253" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105579 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of ​​the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is $24\\sqrt{2} \\text{ cm}^2.$ Find $AB.$\n\n![](images/e9/e93e732bd04cfda304c2098087548a638d8d869ccf8b1050235e8207825875d3.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105579 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c8d31ea4-41c6-5f24-bf7a-a950a30342d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105579 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\dfrac{1}{2}bc\\sin{A},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\angle{A}$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB, \\\\ b & =AC=12\\text{ cm}, \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\sin{45^{\\circ}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $c=AB,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 24\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\ 24\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot c\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ 24\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}} & =3\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}c \\\\ 24 & =3c \\\\ c & =8\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AB = 8\\,\\textrm{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186295109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186295110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186295123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/